WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.870
Film Courage: Harvey, when you get a call
from someone to take on a project or consider

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.029
a project, what are some of the first things
you do to make sure you want to commit?

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:13.150
Harvey Mason, Jr.: First of all, I look and
see if I have kind of the bandwidth to do

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.120
it, the scheduling and it’s something I
can fit into my workload.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.340
And then, more importantly, I look at it from
the standpoint of is it something that is

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.980
going to be really, really good, high quality,
something that I am going to have fun doing,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.440
something that I am not going to be coming
into the studio everyday thinking “Oh God,

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.960
what am I working on?”

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.100
In this point in my career, I want to do great
things.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:39.940
I want to do projects that I am proud of and
that I enjoy creating.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:45.600
Film Courage: Definitely with SING movie,
I can see why you would want to take that

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.600
project on.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.030
Harvey Mason, Jr.: SING was a no-brainer for
me because it was so much great music, it

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:57.299
was working with an amazing cast and one of
my favorite director/studio head combinations

00:00:57.299 --> 00:00:59.859
that I’ve ever worked with, Chris Meledandri
and Garth Jennings.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.899
Those guys are just amazing.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:06.579
I always love working with Mike Knobloch at
Universal because he’s a real musical guy

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:11.460
and when he calls me with a project, usually
it’s something exciting.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.250
He’s really intense about music and he loves
it.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:21.270
When he calls and says “Hey I’ve got a
potential project” I stop what I am doing

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.410
and concentrate on what he’s talking about
because it’s usually something fun.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:30.170
Straight Outta Compton I did for him, the
James Brown film GET ON UP I did for him and

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.640
so just real music intensive projects.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.659
Film Courage: In another interview you did
with Jennifer Hudson for SING, you two talked

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.280
about how some of the music is much older
and a millennials unfortunately may not know

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.290
some of these artists, so I think that is
great that you are bringing these older artists

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.290
into the movie.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:50.400
Harvey Mason, Jr.: That has been my goal with
some of this music is to freshen it up a little

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.820
bit so that young people can enjoy listening
to it.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.821
Maybe it will make them go back and listen
to some of the music that this artist has

00:01:57.821 --> 00:01:59.070
done before.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.439
It introduces it in ways they can digest.

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:07.750
Film Courage: Going back to something you
said earlier about wanting to enjoy what you

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:12.050
are working on, I know you are at a point
in your career where you can probably be choosey.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.030
This is certainly an excellent place to be.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:19.970
What is it about a project or how do you decide
“You know what, I think maybe this won’t

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:21.030
be for me?”

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.290
Was this always the case for you?

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.640
Were you always at a place where you thought
“I don’t want to work on this because

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.080
I won’t be committed 100%” Harvey Mason,
Jr.: (Laughs) No, I wasn’t always at that

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.750
place because I used to commit to everything
100% and it got really draining and tiring,

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.560
taxing my creativity.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.300
For years and years, we were doing records
with artists that were not great artists or

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.680
artists that you might not think would come
out.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.930
A lot of them didn’t come out.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.010
We wasted a lot of really good material and
songs on artists that we really shouldn’t

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.010
have been working on.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.860
But at the early stages of my career, I just
wanted to create.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:55.940
I wanted to be in the studio.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:02.660
I wanted to be working on my craft, so I used
a lot of years doing things that I otherwise

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:03.660
wouldn’t have done.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.680
I think it is all a progression.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.710
It goes in stages.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.610
One thing leads to another and gets me to
where I am.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:13.450
I worked with artists that were new.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.590
I didn’t know if they would come out, but
it ended up that I worked with one of them

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.450
and it was Chris Brown, who was 14-years-old
when I started with him.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.880
At that time we were doing a ton of artists
for Jai Records.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.480
A lot of them were new or unsigned.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.471
At that time you never know what is going
to hit, what is not going to hit and he [Chris

00:03:28.471 --> 00:03:32.270
Brown] ended up being a kind of a career artist
for me and somebody that I have done tons

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:33.520
of songs with.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:39.000
So early in my career I definitely took the
approach of work with everybody, give it your

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.500
all, pour everything you have into every project.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:47.210
Now (as you’ve said) I’m a little more
selective and I concentrate on stuff that

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:48.450
I think is going to be great.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:51.100
Film Courage: I think you started out with
jingles in the beginning?

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.600
Harvey Mason, Jr.: I did.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.410
Film Courage: Let’s suppose for someone
starting out, whether it is in music producing,

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:01.061
film, acting, whatever – should they take
on every project that they can at first, but

00:04:01.061 --> 00:04:05.040
then once you get to a certain level, choose
which ones are going to fulfill you instead

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.040
of drain you?

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.040
Harvey Mason, Jr.: Absolutely.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.340
And I would say no project is too small.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.820
You can learn from every project.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.180
You can improve from every project.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:16.290
You can also build your name and reputation
from every project.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:22.860
Early on in your career, I would recommend
anyone in the arts be it acting, creating,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.390
anything…start with doing everything you
can.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.510
Get your hands on any project because you
can always improve, you can always get better,

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.320
you can always build your reputation and your
name.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.370
You don’t know who is going to hear that
song you wrote for that horrible artist and

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.840
then it leads you to a great artist.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.330
And also, you don’t know what you’re going
to pick up from any given session, any given

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:42.330
artist.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.640
You can learn one of the things that can last
you an entire career.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.390
I learned so many things early in my studio
sessions that have stayed with me forever,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.920
all the way to this point to where I do what
I do now.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:59.770
A lot of what I do now I learned early on
in my career, so that’s a formative time

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.350
in your creative process.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.280
I don’t think at that point you can afford
to be choosy.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:09.110
I see a lot of people doing that like “Oh,
I’m not going to work on that” and I think

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.389
that’s a huge mistake.

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:17.900
Because there is always some tiny nugget you
are going to get from everyday you show up

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.330
to work in the studio.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:25.210
It’s repetition…you have to do it hundreds
and hundreds of songs to get to that place

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:25.819
where you are creating great ones.

